/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ai-900.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ai-900", [["aif1",0,1,"A retailer wants to estimate how many umbrellas each store will sell next week using past sales and the weather forecast. Which AI workload is this?",["Anomaly detection","Computer vision","Prediction and forecasting","Knowledge mining"],2,"Estimating a future quantity from historical data is prediction or forecasting, usually built with a regression model. Anomaly detection looks for unusual values rather than estimating future ones.","Identify features of common AI workloads",["Anomaly detection flags unusual values against a normal pattern; it does not estimate a future sales quantity.","Computer vision interprets images and video, and this scenario uses sales and weather data, not pictures.",null,"Knowledge mining makes large volumes of documents searchable; it does not forecast demand."],1],["aif2",0,1,"A bank wants to flag card transactions that look very different from a customer's normal spending so they can be reviewed. Which workload fits best?",["Anomaly detection","Speech recognition","Generative AI","Object detection"],0,"Anomaly detection learns what normal looks like and flags unusual events such as fraudulent transactions. Generative AI creates new content and is not the core technique for spotting outliers.","Identify features of common AI workloads",[null,"Speech recognition converts spoken audio into text and has nothing to do with card transaction data.","Generative AI creates new content such as text or images; spotting unusual transactions is a detection task.","Object detection locates objects in images, not unusual patterns in payment records."],1],["aif3",0,1,"A city council wants software that reads number plates from traffic camera images. Which AI workload does this use?",["Natural language processing","Computer vision","Anomaly detection","Conversational AI"],1,"Reading text from images is computer vision (specifically optical character recognition). NLP works on text that is already digital, not on pixels in an image.","Identify computer vision workloads",["NLP works on text that is already digital; reading characters from a camera image is a vision task first.",null,"Anomaly detection flags unusual events in data, but reading plate characters is text extraction from images.","Conversational AI powers bots that hold dialogs with users, not reading plates from traffic images."],1],["aif4",0,1,"A legal firm has thousands of scanned contracts and wants staff to search them by party names, dates and clauses. Which workload describes this?",["Speech synthesis","Regression","Anomaly detection","Knowledge mining"],3,"Knowledge mining extracts information from large volumes of unstructured content (often with OCR and entity extraction) and builds a searchable index. Regression predicts numbers and does not make content searchable.","Identify knowledge mining and document processing workloads",["Speech synthesis turns text into spoken audio and does not make scanned contracts searchable.","Regression predicts a numeric value; it does not extract or index information from documents.","Anomaly detection finds unusual data points, not names, dates and clauses across a document collection.",null],1],["aif5",0,1,"Which statement best describes how a machine learning model differs from traditional rule-based software?",["It runs only in the cloud, whereas rule-based software runs on devices","It always returns the correct answer because it was trained on real data","It learns the relationship between inputs and outputs from example data","It needs no data because a developer writes its decision rules by hand"],2,"A model learns patterns from training data instead of following hand-written rules. Its outputs are probabilistic, so it can be wrong, and models can run in the cloud or on devices.","Identify features of common AI workloads",["Where software runs is not the difference; models can run in the cloud or on devices, and so can rule-based code.","Models give probability-based predictions and can be wrong, even when trained on real data.",null,"This describes rule-based software, the opposite of a model that learns its logic from example data."],2],["aif6",0,1,"A support team wants a tool that drafts a polite reply to each customer email, which an agent edits before sending. Which workload is this?",["Generative AI","Image classification","Anomaly detection","Knowledge mining"],0,"Creating new text from a prompt (the customer email) is generative AI. Knowledge mining indexes existing content for search but does not write new replies.","Identify features of generative AI workloads",[null,"Image classification assigns labels to images; the task here is writing new text replies to emails.","Anomaly detection flags unusual events; it does not draft replies to customers.","Knowledge mining indexes existing content for search but does not write new, tailored replies."],1],["aif7",0,1,"A loan approval model rejects applicants from one postcode far more often than equally qualified applicants elsewhere. Which responsible AI principle is most directly at risk?",["Transparency","Inclusiveness","Reliability and safety","Fairness"],3,"Fairness means similar people should get similar outcomes, without bias tied to attributes such as location used as a proxy for ethnicity. Inclusiveness is about designing for everyone to be able to use the system, which is a different concern.","Describe considerations for fairness in an AI solution",["Transparency is about explaining how the system works; the core problem here is unequal outcomes for similar applicants.","Inclusiveness is about everyone being able to use the system, not about biased decisions between groups.","Reliability and safety concerns consistent, safe operation, not systematically different outcomes for one postcode.",null],1],["aif8",0,1,"A company designs its voice assistant so it also works with a screen reader and supports users with speech impairments. Which principle does this demonstrate?",["Accountability","Inclusiveness","Transparency","Privacy and security"],1,"Inclusiveness means empowering everyone and engaging people of all abilities. Accountability is about people being answerable for how the system operates, not accessibility.","Describe considerations for inclusiveness in an AI solution",["Accountability is about who answers for the system through governance, not about accessibility for users.",null,"Transparency concerns explaining how the system works, not making it usable by people with disabilities.","Privacy and security is about protecting data, not about designing for screen readers and speech impairments."],1],["aif9",0,1,"An autonomous warehouse robot is tested extensively under unusual conditions and has a safe fallback if its sensors fail. Which principle is being applied?",["Reliability and safety","Fairness","Inclusiveness","Transparency"],0,"Reliability and safety means the system performs as intended, including in unexpected conditions, and fails safely. Transparency concerns explaining how the system works, not how it behaves under failure.","Describe considerations for reliability and safety in an AI solution",[null,"Fairness concerns equal outcomes across groups of people, not how a robot behaves when sensors fail.","Inclusiveness is about making systems usable by everyone, not about testing edge conditions and failing safely.","Transparency concerns explaining the system's behavior, not engineering a safe fallback for failures."],1],["aif10",0,1,"Customers can see which factors most influenced an AI-driven insurance quote and read documentation on the model's limitations. Which principle does this support?",["Accountability","Reliability and safety","Transparency","Fairness"],2,"Transparency means people understand how an AI system works and what influences its outputs. Accountability is about who is responsible for the system, and fairness about equal treatment.","Describe considerations for transparency in an AI solution",["Accountability is about people being answerable for the system; showing decision factors is about understanding it.","Reliability and safety concerns consistent and safe performance, not explaining which factors drove a quote.",null,"Fairness concerns equal treatment of groups; disclosing influencing factors and limitations is transparency."],2],["aif11",0,1,"An organization sets up a review board that signs off AI systems before release and owns the response when they cause harm. Which principle is this?",["Transparency","Accountability","Inclusiveness","Privacy and security"],1,"Accountability means the people who design and deploy AI are answerable for it, through governance such as review boards and clear ownership. Transparency is about explaining behavior, not assigning responsibility.","Describe considerations for accountability in an AI solution",["Transparency is about explaining how the system works, not assigning ownership and sign-off responsibility.",null,"Inclusiveness is about designing for all users, not about governance and answering for harm.","Privacy and security is about protecting data, not about a board that owns decisions and their consequences."],2],["aif12",0,1,"A health app's model is trained on patient records. The team removes names, encrypts the data and limits who can access it. Which principle is being addressed?",["Fairness","Transparency","Inclusiveness","Privacy and security"],3,"Protecting personal data and controlling access supports the privacy and security principle. Fairness concerns biased outcomes, which anonymization alone does not guarantee.","Describe considerations for privacy and security in an AI solution",["Fairness concerns equal outcomes for groups; removing names and encrypting data protects personal information.","Transparency is about explaining the system, not about encryption and access control.","Inclusiveness is about accessibility for all users, not about protecting patient records.",null],1],["aif13",0,1,"Why does Microsoft require an application and approval before customers can use face identification in Azure AI Face?",["Face identification is still in preview and has no pricing yet","It is a Limited Access feature because misuse could cause serious harm","Face identification only works in regions that need special contracts","Approval is needed to raise the free tier's monthly transaction limit"],1,"Face identification and verification are Limited Access features: Microsoft reviews the customer and use case because of the potential for harm such as surveillance. The requirement is about responsible use, not pricing, preview status or quotas.","Identify guiding principles for responsible AI",["The requirement is not about preview status or pricing; it exists because of the potential for harmful misuse.",null,"Face identification is not restricted to special-contract regions; the approval is about responsible use.","Limited Access approval does not raise free-tier quotas; it gates sensitive capabilities by customer and use case."],2],["aif14",0,1,"A mobile app lets users photograph a plant and tells them its species. Which workload is this?",["Image classification","Speech recognition","Anomaly detection","Knowledge mining"],0,"Assigning one label (the species) to a whole image is image classification, a computer vision workload. Anomaly detection looks for unusual data points, not categories of images.","Identify computer vision workloads",[null,"Speech recognition converts audio to text, but the input here is a photo of a plant.","Anomaly detection flags unusual data points, not which species a photo shows.","Knowledge mining indexes large sets of documents for search, not labeling a single photo."],1],["aif15",0,1,"A call centre wants live captions of phone calls and a bot that answers routine questions by voice. Which workloads are involved?",["Computer vision and knowledge mining","Anomaly detection and regression","Speech and conversational AI","Document processing and forecasting"],2,"Captions need speech to text, and a voice bot needs conversational AI (understanding requests and replying, often with text to speech). None of the other pairs deal with audio or dialog.","Identify natural language processing workloads",["Computer vision and knowledge mining work with images and document search, not phone audio and dialog.","Anomaly detection and regression find outliers and predict numbers; neither transcribes calls or answers by voice.",null,"Document processing and forecasting extract form fields and predict values, not captions or voice bots."],1],["aif16",0,1,"An accounts team wants to pull the vendor name, invoice total and due date out of PDF invoices automatically. Which workload is this?",["Generative AI","Anomaly detection","Speech synthesis","Document processing"],3,"Document processing (document intelligence) extracts structured fields and values from forms and documents such as invoices. Generative AI creates new content rather than reliably extracting existing fields.","Identify document processing workloads",["Generative AI creates new content; reliably pulling existing fields from invoices is a document processing task.","Anomaly detection flags unusual values but does not extract vendor names and totals from PDFs.","Speech synthesis produces spoken audio from text and does not read invoices.",null],1],["aif17",0,1,"A team publishes a document describing their AI system's intended uses, its limitations and how it was tested. Which kind of Microsoft artifact is this modeled on?",["A service level agreement","A transparency note","A data processing addendum","A Limited Access form"],1,"Microsoft publishes transparency notes for its AI services describing capabilities, intended uses and limitations. An SLA covers uptime commitments, not how the AI behaves.","Identify guiding principles for responsible AI",["An SLA commits to uptime and service credits, not to describing an AI system's intended uses and limitations.",null,"A data processing addendum is a contractual privacy document, not a description of how an AI system behaves.","A Limited Access form is an application to use restricted features, not published documentation of limitations."],3],["aif18",0,2,"A dataset of used cars has columns for mileage, age, engine size and sale price. You want to predict sale price. What is the sale price column?",["A feature","The label","A cluster","A hyperparameter"],1,"The label is the value the model learns to predict. Mileage, age and engine size are features, the inputs the model uses to make the prediction.","Describe features and labels in a dataset",["Features are the inputs such as mileage and age; the value being predicted is the label.",null,"A cluster is a group found by unsupervised learning, not a column you are trying to predict.","Hyperparameters are training settings chosen by the data scientist, not a column in the dataset."],1],["aif19",0,2,"A utility company wants to predict the number of kilowatt-hours a household will use next month. Which type of machine learning is this?",["Regression","Binary classification","Clustering","Multiclass classification"],0,"Predicting a numeric value such as energy use is regression. Classification predicts a category, and clustering groups unlabeled items.","Identify regression machine learning scenarios",[null,"Binary classification predicts one of two categories, but energy use is a continuous number.","Clustering groups unlabeled items and does not predict a numeric value for each household.","Multiclass classification picks one of several categories, while kilowatt-hours are a numeric quantity."],2],["aif20",0,2,"A hospital wants to predict whether a patient will be readmitted within 30 days: yes or no. Which technique fits?",["Regression","Clustering","Binary classification","Multiclass classification"],2,"Two possible outcomes make this binary classification. Multiclass classification would apply if there were three or more categories, such as low, medium or high risk.","Identify classification machine learning scenarios",["Regression predicts a number, but readmitted yes or no is a category with two values.","Clustering is unsupervised grouping with no known labels, while readmission outcomes are known labels.",null,"Multiclass classification needs three or more classes; yes or no is only two."],1],["aif21",0,2,"A marketing team has purchase histories but no predefined customer categories, and wants to discover groups of customers who behave alike. Which technique should they use?",["Regression","Binary classification","Multiclass classification","Clustering"],3,"Clustering is unsupervised: it finds groups of similar items in data with no labels. Classification needs labeled examples of each known category.","Identify clustering machine learning scenarios",["Regression predicts a numeric value and needs a label, but here there are no categories or targets at all.","Binary classification needs labeled examples of two known classes, which the team does not have.","Multiclass classification needs predefined labeled categories, and the team wants to discover groups instead.",null],1],["aif22",0,2,"A support system must route each ticket to one of five teams: billing, shipping, returns, technical or account. Which technique is this?",["Multiclass classification","Regression","Clustering","Binary classification"],0,"Choosing one label from more than two known classes is multiclass classification. Clustering would be used if the categories were not known in advance.","Identify classification machine learning scenarios",[null,"Regression predicts a number, not which of five named teams should receive a ticket.","Clustering discovers unknown groups; here the five teams are known categories to predict.","Binary classification has only two classes, while this task has five."],3],["aif23",0,2,"A model scores 99% accuracy on its training data but only 62% on validation data. What is the most likely explanation?",["The model is underfitting because it is too simple","The validation set was used to train the model too","The model is overfitting and has memorized the training data","The label column was accidentally removed from the data"],2,"A large gap between training and validation performance is the classic sign of overfitting. If validation data had been used for training, the scores would look similar rather than diverge, and underfitting shows poor scores on both.","Describe core machine learning concepts",["Underfitting gives poor scores on both training and validation data, not 99% on training.","If validation data had been used for training, validation scores would look high too, not far lower.",null,"A missing label column would stop supervised training altogether rather than produce this score gap."],3],["aif24",0,2,"Why do data scientists split their data into training and validation sets?",["To reduce the cost of storing the data in Azure","To evaluate the model on data it did not see during training","To make training faster by using only half of the features","To create labels for data that was originally unlabeled"],1,"Holding back validation data shows how well the model generalizes to new data. The split does not create labels or remove features.","Describe core machine learning concepts",["Splitting data does not reduce storage cost; all of the data is still stored.",null,"The split is by rows, not by features; every feature is still used in training.","Splitting data cannot create labels; supervised training needs labels before the split."],1],["aif25",0,2,"You evaluate a regression model that predicts house prices. Which metric tells you the proportion of the variance in prices that the model explains, where closer to 1 is better?",["Precision","Recall","Mean absolute error","Coefficient of determination (R²)"],3,"R² measures how much of the variance in the label the model explains, with 1 being a perfect fit. MAE is an average error in the label's units, and precision and recall are classification metrics.","Describe regression evaluation metrics",["Precision is a classification metric about predicted positives and does not apply to price predictions.","Recall is a classification metric about found positives, not variance explained in a numeric label.","MAE is the average error in the label's units; it does not express a proportion of variance explained.",null],3],["aif26",0,2,"A fraud model must catch as many real fraud cases as possible, even if some legitimate transactions are flagged for review. Which metric should be prioritized?",["Recall","Precision","R²","Root mean squared error"],0,"Recall measures the share of actual positives (real fraud) that the model finds. Precision measures how many flagged cases were really fraud, which matters more when false alarms are costly.","Describe classification evaluation metrics",[null,"Precision matters when false alarms are costly; here missing real fraud is the bigger concern.","R² is a regression metric and does not measure how many fraud cases a classifier catches.","RMSE is a regression error metric, not a measure of how many positives a classifier finds."],3],["aif27",0,2,"In a binary classification confusion matrix, what is a false positive?",["A negative case the model correctly predicted as negative","A positive case the model wrongly predicted as negative","A positive case the model correctly predicted as positive","A negative case the model wrongly predicted as positive"],3,"A false positive is a prediction of positive for a case that is actually negative, such as flagging a legitimate email as spam. A positive case predicted as negative is a false negative.","Describe classification evaluation metrics",["A negative case correctly predicted as negative is a true negative.","A positive case wrongly predicted as negative is a false negative, the opposite mistake.","A positive case correctly predicted as positive is a true positive.",null],3],["aif28",0,2,"Which kind of model is best suited to learning complex patterns in images, audio and natural language?",["A linear regression model","A deep neural network","A k-means clustering model","A decision table of hand-written rules"],1,"Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers that learn features directly from raw data such as pixels and audio, which is why modern vision, speech and language models use it. Linear regression cannot capture those complex patterns.","Describe deep learning concepts",["Linear regression fits simple straight-line relationships and cannot learn complex patterns in pixels or audio.",null,"K-means only groups similar items; it does not learn the layered features needed for images and language.","Hand-written rule tables cannot cover the endless variation in images, speech and natural language."],3],["aif29",0,2,"In the transformer architecture used by modern language models, what does the attention mechanism do?",["It converts audio into text before the model processes it","It removes stop words so that the input is shorter","It weighs how relevant each token is to the others in the sequence","It checks model output for harmful content before returning it"],2,"Attention lets the model consider the relationships between tokens, so the meaning of a word depends on its context. Content checks are done by separate safety systems, not by attention.","Describe features of the transformer architecture",["Converting audio to text is speech recognition, a separate step, not what attention does inside a transformer.","Attention does not delete words; it weighs relationships among all the tokens in the input.",null,"Checking output for harmful content is done by a separate safety system such as content filters."],3],["aif30",0,2,"A data scientist needs a single managed virtual machine to run Jupyter notebooks while exploring data in Azure Machine Learning. What should they create?",["A compute instance","A compute cluster","An online endpoint","A data asset"],0,"A compute instance is a managed development VM for notebooks. A compute cluster scales out for training jobs, and an online endpoint serves a deployed model.","Describe capabilities of Azure Machine Learning",[null,"A compute cluster scales out for training jobs; it is not a single personal VM for notebooks.","An online endpoint serves predictions from a deployed model, not an interactive notebook environment.","A data asset is a reference to data, not compute that can run notebooks."],2],["aif31",0,2,"An analyst with no coding experience wants Azure Machine Learning to try many algorithms on a labeled dataset and pick the best classification model. What should they use?",["The designer with a custom Python script","A compute instance running a notebook","Automated machine learning","A batch endpoint with a registered model"],2,"Automated ML tries multiple algorithms and settings and ranks the models by a chosen metric, with no code needed. Notebooks and custom scripts require coding, and an endpoint only serves a model you already have.","Describe capabilities of automated machine learning",["The designer requires choosing the algorithm yourself, and a custom Python script means writing code.","Running a notebook on a compute instance requires writing training code.",null,"A batch endpoint scores data with a model you already have; it does not train and compare algorithms."],2],["aif32",0,2,"A team wants to build a training pipeline visually by dragging data, transformation and training modules onto a canvas. Which Azure Machine Learning feature fits?",["Automated machine learning","The designer","Data labeling","The model catalog"],1,"The designer is a drag-and-drop canvas for building pipelines. Automated ML chooses algorithms for you but does not give you a module-by-module canvas.","Describe capabilities of Azure Machine Learning",["Automated ML picks and compares algorithms for you rather than giving a canvas of modules to connect.",null,"Data labeling is for tagging data with labels, not for building training pipelines.","The model catalog is for discovering and deploying existing models, not building a visual training pipeline."],2],["aif33",0,2,"A trained model must return a prediction within a second whenever a web app sends it one customer's data. How should it be deployed?",["As a batch endpoint that scores files on a schedule","As a data asset registered in the workspace","As a compute cluster that scales to zero nodes","As a real-time (online) endpoint"],3,"Online endpoints serve low-latency predictions for individual requests. Batch endpoints score large volumes of data asynchronously, and a compute cluster alone does not expose a prediction API.","Describe model deployment in Azure Machine Learning",["Batch endpoints process large volumes asynchronously, which is too slow for a one-second response per request.","Registering a data asset records data; it does not make a model available for predictions.","A compute cluster runs training or batch jobs and does not by itself expose a prediction endpoint.",null],2],["aif34",0,2,"After training a model in Azure Machine Learning, a team wants to see whether its error rate is higher for some groups of people than others. Which tool helps?",["The Responsible AI dashboard","Automated ML featurization","A compute cluster's autoscale settings","The workspace's Key Vault"],0,"The Responsible AI dashboard brings together error analysis, fairness assessment, explanations and more to find where a model performs worse. Featurization prepares data but does not assess fairness.","Describe responsible AI capabilities in Azure Machine Learning",[null,"Featurization prepares data for AutoML and does not assess how errors differ between groups.","Autoscale settings control the number of nodes, not whether the model treats groups differently.","Key Vault stores secrets and keys; it has no model fairness or error analysis features."],3],["aif35",0,3,"A quality control system must find every scratch on a product photo and return the location of each one. Which computer vision technique is required?",["Image classification","Object detection","Optical character recognition","Image captioning"],1,"Object detection returns a label and bounding box for each object found, so it can locate multiple scratches. Image classification gives a single label for the whole image with no locations.","Identify common types of computer vision solution",["Image classification gives one label for the whole image and cannot locate each scratch.",null,"OCR extracts text from images; scratches are visual defects, not characters.","Image captioning writes a sentence about the whole image and does not return the location of each scratch."],2],["aif36",0,3,"A medical imaging tool must mark exactly which pixels in a scan belong to a tumour, not just draw a box around it. Which technique is this?",["Object detection","Image classification","Semantic segmentation","Facial analysis"],2,"Semantic segmentation classifies each pixel, giving the precise shape of a region. Object detection only draws a rectangular bounding box around each object.","Identify common types of computer vision solution",["Object detection draws a rectangular box around the tumour rather than marking exactly which pixels belong to it.","Image classification labels the whole scan and gives no location or shape information.",null,"Facial analysis works on faces, not medical scans."],3],["aif37",0,3,"A photo-sharing app wants to generate a one-sentence description of each uploaded photo for users of screen readers. Which Azure AI Vision capability fits?",["Image captions","Smart crops","OCR (Read)","Face detection"],0,"Image analysis captions produce a human-readable sentence describing the image, which is ideal for alt text. Smart crops only choose a thumbnail region, and OCR only extracts text.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Vision service",[null,"Smart crops only choose a thumbnail region; they do not produce a description.","OCR extracts text that appears in the image rather than describing the scene.","Face detection locates faces but does not describe the whole photo in a sentence."],1],["aif38",0,3,"An insurer wants to extract handwritten and printed text from photos of damage claim forms, line by line. Which feature should it use?",["Image tagging","Smart crops","Face detection","OCR with the Read feature"],3,"The Read (OCR) feature extracts printed and handwritten text as lines and words with their positions. Tagging describes visual content but does not return the text on the page.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Vision service",["Image tagging returns keywords describing the scene, not the written text on the form.","Smart crops suggest a thumbnail region and do not extract any text.","Face detection finds faces and has nothing to do with reading form text.",null],2],["aif39",0,3,"What does a convolutional neural network (CNN) use to identify visual features such as edges and shapes in an image?",["Grammar rules applied to pixel names","Filters that are learned during training","Tokens that are looked up in a dictionary","A fixed list of colors defined by the developer"],1,"CNNs apply filters (kernels) across the image; the filter values are learned in training so the network picks out edges, textures and shapes. They are not hand-written rules or color lists.","Identify common types of computer vision solution",["CNNs do not use grammar rules or pixel names; they apply numeric filters to pixel values.",null,"Token dictionaries relate to language models, not how CNNs detect edges and shapes.","CNN features are learned, not a fixed color list chosen by a developer."],3],["aif40",0,3,"A retailer wants to count how many people are in each frame from a store camera, without identifying anyone. Which capability fits best?",["Face identification","OCR with the Read feature","People detection in image analysis","Custom text classification"],2,"People detection finds people in an image and returns their bounding boxes, which is enough to count them without recognizing anyone. Face identification recognizes specific individuals and needs Limited Access approval.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Vision service",["Face identification recognizes specific people, needs Limited Access and goes beyond simply counting.","OCR extracts text, and the goal is to count people, not read signs.",null,"Custom text classification labels documents, not people in camera images."],2],["aif41",0,3,"A developer wants to detect faces in photos and return whether each face is blurred or wearing glasses. Which service should they use?",["Azure AI Document Intelligence","Azure AI Language","Azure AI Translator","Azure AI Face"],3,"Azure AI Face detects faces and returns attributes such as blur, glasses, head pose and occlusion. Document Intelligence extracts data from documents and has no face attributes.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Face detection service",["Document Intelligence extracts data from documents and does not return face attributes like blur or glasses.","Azure AI Language analyzes text, not images of faces.","Azure AI Translator translates text between languages and has no face analysis.",null],2],["aif42",0,3,"A company wants employees to unlock a door by matching a live camera image to their stored photo using Azure AI Face. What must the company do first?",["Apply for and receive approval under the Limited Access policy","Switch the Face resource from the F0 tier to the S0 tier","Train a custom image classification model for each employee","Enable the emotion recognition attribute on the resource"],0,"Face verification and identification are Limited Access features that require Microsoft's approval of the customer and use case. Emotion recognition was retired, and a pricing tier change does not unlock restricted features.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Face detection service",[null,"Moving from F0 to S0 changes pricing and limits but does not unlock Limited Access features.","Face verification uses the Face service's recognition features; training an image classifier per employee is not the approach.","Emotion recognition was retired from Azure AI Face and is unrelated to matching a person to their photo."],2],["aif43",0,3,"A company processes thousands of supplier invoices and needs the invoice number, line items and totals as structured data. Which service fits best with the least effort?",["Azure AI Vision image tagging","Azure AI Document Intelligence prebuilt invoice model","Azure AI Language key phrase extraction","Azure AI Face detection"],1,"The prebuilt invoice model in Document Intelligence returns invoice fields and line items as structured data without training. Key phrase extraction works on plain text and does not understand invoice fields.","Describe capabilities of Azure AI Document Intelligence",["Image tagging describes what an image shows and does not return invoice numbers or line items.",null,"Key phrase extraction lists main topics in plain text; it does not understand invoice fields or tables.","Face detection finds faces in images and has no use for invoices."],1],["aif44",0,3,"A company's internal onboarding form has a unique layout that no prebuilt model covers. How can Document Intelligence extract its fields?",["Use the prebuilt receipt model and ignore missing fields","Convert each form to audio and use speech to text","Train a custom model using labeled sample forms","Run image captioning and parse the caption text"],2,"Custom models learn to extract fields from your own document types from a small set of labeled samples. The receipt model only understands receipts, and captions describe images rather than extract form fields.","Describe capabilities of Azure AI Document Intelligence",["The receipt model only understands receipts and would miss the onboarding form's own fields.","Converting forms to audio makes no sense; speech to text transcribes speech, not document layouts.",null,"Captions describe an image in a sentence and do not extract named form fields reliably."],3],["aif45",0,3,"Which Document Intelligence model extracts text, tables, selection marks and document structure from any document without looking for specific named fields?",["The layout model","The prebuilt ID document model","The prebuilt invoice model","A custom classification model"],0,"The layout model returns text, tables, selection marks and structure for any document. The invoice and ID models look for specific fields in those document types.","Describe capabilities of Azure AI Document Intelligence",[null,"The ID document model looks for specific identity fields such as name and date of birth.","The invoice model looks for invoice-specific fields such as vendor and total.","A custom classification model identifies the document type; it does not extract text and tables."],3],["aif46",0,3,"A developer wants one endpoint and one key to call Vision, Language and Translator from an app, with a single bill. What should they create?",["A separate single-service resource for each service","An Azure Machine Learning workspace","A compute cluster with three nodes","A multi-service Azure AI services resource"],3,"A multi-service Azure AI services resource provides one endpoint and key for several AI services with consolidated billing. Single-service resources each have their own endpoint and key.","Identify Azure tools and services for computer vision tasks",["Separate single-service resources each have their own endpoint and keys, which is the opposite of the requirement.","An Azure Machine Learning workspace is for training your own models, not calling Vision, Language and Translator.","A compute cluster runs training jobs; it provides no AI service endpoints or keys.",null],1],["aif47",0,3,"A student wants to try Azure AI Vision for a class project at no cost and accepts low transaction limits. What should they choose?",["A multi-service resource on a paid tier","A single-service Vision resource on the free F0 tier","A compute instance with a GPU","A Premium App Service plan"],1,"Single-service resources such as Azure AI Vision offer a free F0 tier with limited transactions where available. A paid multi-service resource bills for every call, and GPU compute is expensive.","Identify Azure tools and services for computer vision tasks",["A paid multi-service tier bills for every call, and the multi-service resource has no free tier.",null,"A GPU compute instance is expensive and is not needed to call a prebuilt vision API.","An App Service plan hosts web apps and does not provide Azure AI Vision capabilities."],1],["aif48",0,3,"When an app calls Azure AI Vision with a key, which two values from the resource does it need?",["The subscription ID and the tenant ID","The resource group name and the region","The endpoint URL and a resource key","The storage account name and a SAS token"],2,"Clients call the resource's endpoint URL and authenticate with one of its two keys (or with Microsoft Entra ID). Subscription and resource group names are not used to call the API.","Identify Azure tools and services for computer vision tasks",["Subscription and tenant IDs identify your Azure account, but the API call itself needs the endpoint and a key.","The resource group and region are management details, not what an app sends to call the API.",null,"Storage account names and SAS tokens are for Azure Storage, not for authenticating to Azure AI Vision."],2],["aif49",0,3,"A news site wants to automatically choose the most interesting region of each photo for square thumbnails. Which image analysis feature helps?",["Smart crops","Dense captions","OCR (Read)","Background removal of text"],0,"Smart crops suggest a crop region around the area of interest for a given aspect ratio. Dense captions describe regions in words but do not produce thumbnail crops.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Vision service",[null,"Dense captions describe regions of an image in words but do not produce thumbnail crops.","OCR extracts text from the image rather than choosing an interesting region.","This is not a real image analysis feature, and removing text does not choose a thumbnail region."],2],["aif50",0,3,"A security team asks for a system that infers the emotional state of shoppers from camera images using Azure AI Face. What should you tell them?",["Emotion inference requires only the free F0 tier","Emotion inference must be enabled with a custom model","Emotion inference is available only in Document Intelligence","Azure AI Face no longer offers emotion recognition"],3,"Microsoft retired emotion, gender and age inference from Azure AI Face as part of its responsible AI commitments. No tier or custom model restores it, and Document Intelligence does not analyze faces.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Face detection service",["No pricing tier, free or paid, restores emotion inference, because the capability was retired.","Emotion inference cannot be re-enabled with a custom model in Azure AI Face; it was withdrawn.","Document Intelligence extracts data from documents and has never analyzed faces or emotions.",null],2],["aif51",0,3,"A newer type of vision model can answer a free-text question about an image, such as 'Is the fire exit blocked?'. What kind of model is this?",["A regression model trained on pixel values","A multimodal model that combines images and language","A clustering model that groups similar photos","A rule-based OCR engine"],1,"Multimodal models are trained on both images and text, so they can describe images and answer questions about them. OCR only extracts text, and clustering only groups images.","Identify common types of computer vision solution",["A regression model predicts a number and cannot answer a free-text question about an image.",null,"A clustering model groups similar images but cannot understand and answer a question.","Rule-based OCR only extracts text and cannot reason about whether an exit is blocked."],2],["aif52",0,4,"A hotel chain wants to know whether each online review is positive, negative or neutral. Which Azure AI Language feature should it use?",["Key phrase extraction","Sentiment analysis","Entity linking","Language detection"],1,"Sentiment analysis returns positive, neutral or negative labels with confidence scores. Key phrase extraction lists the main talking points but does not judge their tone.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Key phrase extraction lists talking points but does not say whether the tone is positive or negative.",null,"Entity linking maps mentions to knowledge base entries and does not judge sentiment.","Language detection identifies which language the review is in, not how the writer feels."],1],["aif53",0,4,"A review says 'The room was spotless but the breakfast was cold.' The hotel wants the sentiment toward 'room' and 'breakfast' separately. What should it enable?",["Language detection","Named entity recognition","Opinion mining","Custom text classification"],2,"Opinion mining (aspect-based sentiment) links opinions to specific targets such as room and breakfast. Document-level sentiment would give only one overall label for mixed text like this.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Language detection only identifies the language, not opinions about the room or breakfast.","NER finds and categorizes entities such as people and places but does not attach sentiment to them.",null,"Custom text classification assigns your own labels to whole documents, not per-aspect sentiment."],2],["aif54",0,4,"A global support inbox receives messages in many languages and must route each one to a team that speaks that language. Which feature identifies the language?",["Language detection","Speech synthesis","Key phrase extraction","Question answering"],0,"Language detection returns the language of the text with a confidence score. Translator can also detect language, but among these options, language detection is the feature designed for this.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",[null,"Speech synthesis produces audio from text and does not identify a message's language.","Key phrase extraction returns main topics, not the language of the text.","Question answering returns answers from a knowledge base and does not route by language."],1],["aif55",0,4,"A news aggregator wants to list the main talking points of each article, such as 'interest rates' or 'housing market'. Which feature fits?",["Sentiment analysis","PII detection","Speech to text","Key phrase extraction"],3,"Key phrase extraction returns the main concepts in a text. Sentiment analysis reports tone, and PII detection finds personal data.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Sentiment analysis reports tone, not the topics an article talks about.","PII detection finds personal data such as phone numbers, not the article's main points.","Speech to text transcribes audio, and these articles are already written text.",null],1],["aif56",0,4,"A law firm needs to find people, organizations, dates and locations mentioned in contracts. Which feature should it use?",["Named entity recognition","Language detection","Opinion mining","Speech translation"],0,"Named entity recognition identifies and categorizes entities such as people, organizations, dates and locations. Opinion mining finds opinions about aspects, not entities.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",[null,"Language detection only identifies the language of the contract, not the entities mentioned in it.","Opinion mining links opinions to aspects; it does not list people, organizations and dates.","Speech translation works on spoken audio, not on extracting entities from written contracts."],1],["aif57",0,4,"Before sharing chat transcripts with a vendor, a company must find and mask phone numbers, email addresses and card numbers. Which feature fits?",["Entity linking","Key phrase extraction","PII detection","Sentiment analysis"],2,"PII detection identifies personal information and can return redacted text. Entity linking connects entities to a knowledge base and does not redact anything.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Entity linking connects mentions to knowledge base entries and does not find or mask phone and card numbers.","Key phrase extraction lists main topics and does not detect or redact personal data.",null,"Sentiment analysis judges tone and does nothing to hide personal information."],2],["aif58",0,4,"A text mentions 'Mercury'. The app must know whether this refers to the planet, the element or the musician, and link to the right reference page. Which feature does this?",["Language detection","Entity linking","Summarization","Custom text classification"],1,"Entity linking disambiguates an entity using context and links it to a knowledge base entry. Summarization shortens text but does not resolve which Mercury is meant.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Language detection says which language the text is in, not which Mercury is meant.",null,"Summarization shortens text but does not resolve an ambiguous entity to a reference page.","Custom text classification labels whole documents with your categories and does not disambiguate a single entity."],3],["aif59",0,4,"A semantic search system finds articles similar in meaning to a query even when they share no words. What does it compare?",["The number of characters in each text","Embedding vectors that represent the meaning of the text","The language code returned by language detection","The order in which words appear in each text"],1,"Embeddings are vectors in which texts with similar meaning sit close together, so similarity can be measured even without shared words. Character counts and word order do not capture meaning.","Describe features of language models",["Character counts say nothing about meaning, so they cannot match texts that use different words.",null,"The language code only tells you which language a text is in, not what it means.","Word order alone cannot match texts that share no words; meaning is captured by embeddings."],3],["aif60",0,4,"A legal team wants a short paragraph that captures the key points of each long court ruling. Which Azure AI Language feature fits?",["Summarization","Key phrase extraction","Language detection","Named entity recognition"],0,"Summarization produces a condensed version of the text, either by selecting key sentences (extractive) or by writing new ones (abstractive). Key phrase extraction returns separate phrases, not a readable paragraph.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",[null,"Key phrase extraction returns a list of separate phrases, not a readable summary paragraph.","Language detection identifies the language and does not condense the ruling.","NER lists entities such as parties and dates, not the key points of the ruling."],3],["aif61",0,4,"A company wants meeting recordings turned into written transcripts. Which capability of Azure AI Speech should it use?",["Text to speech","Speaker voice creation","Language detection of text","Speech to text"],3,"Speech to text (speech recognition) converts spoken audio into text. Text to speech does the opposite, producing audio from text.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Text to speech goes the other way, producing audio from text rather than transcripts from audio.","Creating a custom synthetic voice produces speech output; it does not transcribe recordings.","Language detection works on text that already exists, not on meeting audio.",null],1],["aif62",0,4,"A navigation app must read directions aloud to drivers in a natural-sounding voice. Which capability should it use?",["Speech to text","Text to speech","Speech translation","Conversational language understanding"],1,"Text to speech (speech synthesis) turns text into spoken audio using neural voices. Speech to text converts audio into text, which is the opposite direction.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Speech to text converts audio into text, the opposite of reading directions aloud.",null,"Speech translation converts speech into another language, which the app does not need.","CLU predicts intents and entities from user input; it does not produce spoken audio."],1],["aif63",0,4,"An e-commerce site must translate product descriptions from English into French, German and Japanese. Which service is designed for this?",["Azure AI Language","Azure AI Speech text to speech","Azure AI Translator","Azure AI Document Intelligence"],2,"Azure AI Translator translates text (and whole documents) between many languages, including into several targets in one request. Azure AI Language analyzes text but does not translate it.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Azure AI Language analyzes text for sentiment, entities and more, but it does not translate.","Text to speech produces audio from text in the same language; it does not translate descriptions.",null,"Document Intelligence extracts fields from documents and does not translate them."],1],["aif64",0,4,"At a conference, a speaker's English speech must appear as live Spanish subtitles. Which capability fits best?",["Speech translation in Azure AI Speech","Document translation in Azure AI Translator","Sentiment analysis in Azure AI Language","Key phrase extraction in Azure AI Language"],0,"Speech translation takes spoken audio and returns translated text (or speech) in real time. Document translation works on files, not live audio.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",[null,"Document translation works on stored files asynchronously, not on live spoken audio.","Sentiment analysis judges tone and does not translate the speech into Spanish.","Key phrase extraction lists main topics and neither transcribes nor translates speech."],3],["aif65",0,4,"A travel app user says 'Book me a flight to Lisbon next Friday'. The app must work out that the user wants to book a flight and capture Lisbon and next Friday. Which service does this?",["Question answering","Conversational language understanding","Azure AI Translator","Key phrase extraction"],1,"Conversational language understanding predicts the intent (BookFlight) and extracts entities (destination, date) from an utterance. Question answering matches questions to stored answers but does not extract entities for an action.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Question answering returns stored answers from a knowledge base; it does not extract a destination and date for an action.",null,"Translator changes the language of text but does not work out what the user wants to do.","Key phrase extraction lists topics but does not identify the user's intent or label entities for booking."],2],["aif66",0,4,"In a conversational language understanding project, what is 'What's the weather in Oslo tomorrow?' an example of?",["An intent","An entity","A label for a regression model","An utterance"],3,"An utterance is an example of something a user might say. The intent is the goal behind it (GetWeather), and Oslo and tomorrow are entities.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["The intent is the goal behind the sentence, such as GetWeather, not the example sentence itself.","An entity is a detail inside the sentence, such as Oslo or tomorrow, not the whole sentence.","CLU does not use regression labels; the example sentence is training input, not a numeric target.",null],1],["aif67",0,4,"A council has a web page of 200 frequently asked questions and wants a bot that answers residents from those pairs. Which feature should it use?",["Custom text classification","Speech synthesis","Question answering","Entity linking"],2,"Question answering builds a knowledge base from existing FAQ content and returns the best matching answer. Text classification assigns labels but does not return answers.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Custom text classification assigns categories to text but does not return answers to questions.","Speech synthesis turns text into audio; it does not find the right answer to a question.",null,"Entity linking maps mentions to knowledge base entries and does not answer residents' questions."],2],["aif68",0,4,"A company wants incoming emails sorted into its own categories: complaint, refund, order change and feedback. No prebuilt model knows these categories. What should it use?",["Custom text classification trained on labeled emails","Prebuilt sentiment analysis","Language detection","Prebuilt named entity recognition"],0,"Custom text classification learns your own categories from labeled examples. Sentiment analysis only reports positive, neutral or negative tone.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",[null,"Prebuilt sentiment analysis only returns positive, neutral or negative, not the company's own categories.","Language detection only identifies the language of each email.","Prebuilt NER finds entities such as people and dates and cannot sort emails into custom categories."],3],["aif69",0,5,"How does a large language model produce a response to a prompt?",["It looks up the answer in a database of stored responses","It copies the closest matching web page it saw in training","It runs hand-written grammar rules for each supported language","It predicts one likely next token at a time based on the text so far"],3,"LLMs generate text by repeatedly predicting a probable next token and appending it. They do not retrieve stored answers or copy pages, which is also why they can produce fluent but wrong statements.","Identify features of generative AI models",["LLMs do not look answers up in a response database; they generate text token by token.","Models do not copy stored web pages; they generate new text from learned probabilities.","LLMs learn patterns from data rather than running hand-written grammar rules per language.",null],2],["aif70",0,5,"A developer notices that the text 'unbelievable' is counted as more than one unit when calculating the size of a prompt. What are these units called?",["Embeddings","Parameters","Tokens","Epochs"],2,"Models process text as tokens, which can be whole words, parts of words or punctuation; limits and billing are counted in tokens. Embeddings are vectors that represent meaning, not the units of text.","Identify features of generative AI models",["Embeddings are vectors that represent meaning, not the units of text counted in a prompt.","Parameters are a model's learned weights, not pieces of the prompt text.",null,"Epochs are complete passes through training data, not units of text."],2],["aif71",0,5,"A sales team uses an assistant built into their CRM that drafts follow-up emails and summarizes account history on request. What is this kind of generative AI app commonly called?",["A knowledge mining index","A regression model","A clustering pipeline","A copilot"],3,"A copilot is a generative AI assistant embedded in an app that helps users with tasks such as drafting and summarizing, while the user stays in control. A regression model only predicts numbers.","Identify common scenarios for generative AI",["A knowledge mining index makes content searchable; it does not draft emails inside a CRM.","A regression model predicts a number and cannot draft or summarize text.","A clustering pipeline groups similar records and does not assist users with writing tasks.",null],1],["aif72",0,5,"A chat app must always answer as a formal assistant for Contoso, refuse off-topic requests and reply in bullet points. Where should these instructions go?",["In the system message","In the temperature setting","In the model's training data","In the content filter configuration"],0,"The system message sets the model's role, rules and output format for the whole conversation. Temperature controls randomness, and retraining the model is not needed for instructions like these.","Identify prompt engineering techniques",[null,"Temperature only controls randomness; it cannot hold role, refusal rules or format instructions.","Retraining the model is unnecessary and expensive for instructions a system message handles.","Content filters block harmful content categories; they do not set persona or bullet-point formatting."],1],["aif73",0,5,"You include two example questions with the exact answer format you want before the user's real question. What is this technique called?",["Grounding with RAG","Zero-shot prompting","Few-shot prompting","Fine-tuning"],2,"Providing a few worked examples in the prompt is few-shot prompting. Zero-shot gives no examples, and fine-tuning changes the model with additional training rather than examples in the prompt.","Identify prompt engineering techniques",["Grounding with RAG adds retrieved source documents, not worked examples of the answer format.","Zero-shot prompting means giving no examples, which is the opposite of this technique.",null,"Fine-tuning changes the model with additional training rather than putting examples in the prompt."],2],["aif74",0,5,"A company's HR chatbot must answer questions using the current employee handbook, which the model never saw in training, without retraining the model. Which approach fits?",["Raise the temperature so the model is more creative","Use a larger model with more parameters","Train a new model from scratch on the handbook","Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) over the handbook"],3,"RAG retrieves relevant handbook passages at question time and adds them to the prompt so the model answers from them. A larger model still has not seen the handbook, and higher temperature increases randomness.","Identify features of generative AI solutions",["Raising temperature adds randomness and makes invented answers more likely, not less.","A larger model still has never seen the current handbook, so it cannot answer from it.","Training a new model from scratch is hugely expensive and would be stale as soon as the handbook changes.",null],3],["aif75",0,5,"In a RAG solution, what usually happens first when a user asks a question?",["The model generates an answer and then searches for sources","Relevant passages are retrieved from an index of your data","The model is fine-tuned on the user's question","The content filter rewrites the question in simpler words"],1,"RAG retrieves relevant content first (often by vector or hybrid search), adds it to the prompt, then the model generates a grounded answer. Fine-tuning is a separate training process and is not done per question.","Identify features of generative AI solutions",["RAG retrieves before generating; generating first would leave the answer ungrounded.",null,"Fine-tuning is a separate training process and is not run for each user question.","Content filters check for harmful content; they do not rewrite questions as a RAG step."],2],["aif76",0,5,"An app extracts dates and totals from text with an LLM and needs consistent, repeatable output. How should temperature be set?",["High, near the maximum, for varied output","It does not matter because temperature only affects images","Low, near 0, for focused and deterministic output","Temperature should be removed from the request entirely"],2,"Low temperature makes the model pick the most likely tokens, giving consistent answers suited to extraction and facts. High temperature increases randomness, which is better for brainstorming and creative writing.","Identify features of generative AI models",["High temperature increases randomness, which works against consistent, repeatable extraction.","Temperature applies to text generation in language models, not only to images.",null,"Removing it just uses the default value, which is not guaranteed to give deterministic output."],2],["aif77",0,5,"A marketing team wants a model to generate brand-new product images from text descriptions. Which kind of model should they deploy?",["An embeddings model","A speech to text model","A named entity recognition model","An image generation model"],3,"Image generation models create images from text prompts. Embeddings models turn text into vectors for search and similarity and cannot produce images.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["An embeddings model outputs vectors for search and similarity, not images.","Speech to text transcribes audio and cannot create product images.","NER finds entities in text and has no image generation capability.",null],1],["aif78",0,5,"To build semantic search over product manuals for a RAG app, you need to convert each passage into a vector. Which model type does this?",["An embeddings model","A GPT chat completion model","An image generation model","A text to speech model"],0,"Embeddings models return vectors that represent meaning, which are stored in a vector index for similarity search. A chat model generates text rather than vectors.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",[null,"A chat completion model generates text responses, not the vectors a semantic index needs.","An image generation model creates pictures, not vectors for text passages.","Text to speech produces audio from text, not vectors for search."],2],["aif79",0,5,"Where in Microsoft Foundry can you compare available models from Microsoft, OpenAI and other providers, read their model cards and choose one to deploy?",["The Responsible AI dashboard","The Azure Machine Learning designer","Document Intelligence Studio","The model catalog"],3,"The model catalog in Microsoft Foundry lists models from many providers with model cards and deployment options. The designer builds ML pipelines, and Document Intelligence Studio is for document models.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["The Responsible AI dashboard assesses trained ML models for errors and fairness; it does not list provider models.","The designer builds ML training pipelines and does not list models from other providers.","Document Intelligence Studio is for document extraction models, not generative models from many providers.",null],1],["aif80",0,5,"A developer wants to try prompts and a system message against a deployed chat model in a browser before writing any code. What should they use?",["An Azure Machine Learning compute cluster","Language Studio's sentiment analysis page","The chat playground in the Foundry portal","The Azure pricing calculator"],2,"The chat playground lets you test a deployed model with system messages, parameters and your own data without code. A compute cluster runs training jobs, not interactive prompt testing.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["A compute cluster runs training jobs and offers no interactive prompt testing.","Sentiment analysis in Language Studio analyzes text tone and cannot test a chat model's system message.",null,"The pricing calculator estimates costs; it does not run prompts against a model."],1],["aif81",0,5,"Which statement about Azure OpenAI models in Microsoft Foundry is correct?",["The models run only on devices and never in Azure","They are Azure-hosted OpenAI models with Azure security and content filtering","They are free to use with no token-based billing","They require you to train each model from scratch on your own data"],1,"Azure OpenAI provides OpenAI models such as GPT chat and embeddings models hosted in Azure, with Azure networking, identity and built-in content filtering. Usage is billed, and the models are pretrained.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["Azure OpenAI models are hosted in Azure and called over an endpoint, not restricted to devices.",null,"Azure OpenAI usage is billed, typically per token; there is no free tier.","The models are pretrained; you use them through prompts and grounding, not by training from scratch."],3],["aif82",0,5,"A help desk solution uses a model that can look up order status by calling an internal API, search the knowledge base and open a ticket to complete a user's request. What is this solution best described as?",["A clustering model","An OCR pipeline","A regression endpoint","An AI agent"],3,"An agent combines a model with instructions, knowledge and tools such as API calls so it can take actions toward a goal. The other options predict numbers, read text or group data and cannot take actions.","Identify features of generative AI solutions",["A clustering model only groups similar data and cannot call APIs or open tickets.","An OCR pipeline extracts text from images and cannot take actions for a user.","A regression endpoint returns numeric predictions and cannot look up orders or complete tasks.",null],1],["aif83",0,5,"Which Microsoft Foundry capability is designed for building, deploying and managing agents that use models together with tools and knowledge sources?",["Azure AI Document Intelligence","Azure AI Translator","Foundry Agent Service","Azure AI Face"],2,"Foundry Agent Service (formerly Azure AI Agent Service) hosts agents that combine a model, instructions, tools and knowledge. The other services are single-purpose AI services.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["Document Intelligence extracts data from documents; it is not a platform for building agents.","Translator translates text and documents; it does not host agents with tools.",null,"Azure AI Face detects and analyzes faces and has nothing to do with building agents."],2],["aif84",0,5,"Microsoft's guidance for responsible generative AI describes four stages. Which order is correct?",["Identify, measure, mitigate, operate","Measure, identify, operate, mitigate","Mitigate, identify, measure, operate","Operate, mitigate, measure, identify"],0,"You first identify potential harms, then measure how often and how severely they occur, then mitigate them in layers, and finally operate the solution responsibly with monitoring and incident response.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",[null,"You cannot measure harms before you have identified them, and mitigation must come before operating.","Mitigating before identifying and measuring means you do not know which harms to address or whether fixes work.","This is the reverse order; operating a system before identifying and mitigating harms is exactly what the process prevents."],3],["aif85",0,5,"A chat app built on an Azure OpenAI deployment must block hateful and violent content in both prompts and responses. What provides this by default?",["The model's temperature setting","Automated machine learning","The Azure pricing tier","Content filters in the safety system"],3,"Azure OpenAI deployments pass prompts and completions through content filters that detect categories such as hate, sexual, violence and self-harm at configurable severity levels. Temperature only affects randomness.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",["Temperature only changes randomness and does not block hateful or violent content.","Automated machine learning trains predictive models and has no role in filtering chat content.","The pricing tier affects cost and limits, not whether harmful content is blocked.",null],2],["aif86",0,5,"A social platform wants to check user-uploaded text and images for harmful content, separately from any chat app. Which service fits?",["Azure AI Language key phrase extraction","Azure AI Content Safety","Azure AI Document Intelligence","Azure Machine Learning designer"],1,"Azure AI Content Safety analyzes text and images for harmful content categories with severity levels, for any app. Key phrase extraction finds topics but does not assess harm.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",["Key phrase extraction lists topics but does not assess text or images for harmful content.",null,"Document Intelligence extracts fields from documents and does not moderate user uploads.","The designer builds ML pipelines and is not a content moderation service."],2],["aif87",0,5,"Which is an example of a mitigation at the system message and grounding layer for a generative AI app?",["Adding a banner that tells users responses are AI-generated","Choosing a different base model from the catalog","Instructing the model to answer only from provided documents","Setting up a monitoring dashboard after release"],2,"Instructions in the system message and grounding with trusted data are the metaprompt and grounding layer. Choosing a model is the model layer, a banner is the user experience layer, and monitoring belongs to operating the solution.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",["A banner disclosing AI-generated content is a user experience layer mitigation.","Choosing a different base model is a model layer mitigation.",null,"Monitoring after release belongs to the operate stage, not the system message and grounding layer."],3],["aif88",0,5,"An evaluation checks whether each answer from a RAG app is supported by the retrieved source documents rather than made up. Which metric is this?",["Groundedness","Fluency","Latency","Recall"],0,"Groundedness measures how well answers are supported by the provided context. Fluency judges how natural the language reads, which says nothing about whether it is supported by sources.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",[null,"Fluency judges how natural the language reads, not whether answers are supported by sources.","Latency measures response time and says nothing about whether answers are supported.","Recall is a classification metric about found positives, not whether generated text is backed by sources."],3],["aif89",0,5,"Before launch, a team deliberately tries to make its chatbot produce harmful or off-policy output so they can fix weaknesses. What is this practice called?",["Load testing","A/B testing","Red teaming","Fine-tuning"],2,"Red teaming means systematically probing an AI system for harmful outputs to identify risks before release. Load testing checks performance under traffic, not safety.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",["Load testing checks performance under heavy traffic, not whether the chatbot can be made to produce harmful output.","A/B testing compares two versions with real users to see which performs better, not probing for harmful behavior.",null,"Fine-tuning further trains a model; it is not a practice of trying to break it."],2],["aif90",0,5,"A chatbot confidently states a refund policy that does not exist. Which term describes this and what is a common mitigation?",["Overfitting; collect more training data for the base model","A hallucination; ground the model in real policy documents","Tokenization; shorten the user's prompt","Bias; switch the model to a higher temperature"],1,"Fabricated but fluent answers are often called hallucinations (ungrounded output). Grounding with RAG over the real policy and instructing the model to answer only from it reduces them; a higher temperature would make it worse.","Identify features of generative AI solutions",["Overfitting is a training issue with custom models; more base-model training data does not fix an invented policy.",null,"Tokenization is how text is split for a model; it does not describe a fabricated answer, and shortening prompts will not fix it.","Bias refers to unfair skew, and a higher temperature would make invented answers more likely, not less."],2],["x1",0,1,"An online store wants a chat window where customers type questions about delivery times and get instant answers at any hour. Which AI workload is this?",["Computer vision","Anomaly detection","Conversational AI","Regression forecasting"],2,"A bot that holds a dialogue with users through typed or spoken messages is conversational AI. Forecasting may sound useful for delivery times, but it does not answer people in a chat.","Domain 1: Conversational AI",["Computer vision works with images and video, not typed questions in a chat window.","Anomaly detection finds unusual data points, such as odd transactions, not customer questions.",null,"Forecasting predicts future numbers from past data; it does not hold a conversation."],1],["x2",0,1,"A company wants software that reads thousands of customer emails and pulls out the main topics people complain about. Which AI workload does this use?",["Natural language processing","Computer vision","Anomaly detection","Image generation"],0,"Understanding and extracting meaning from written language is natural language processing. Anomaly detection might spot a spike in complaints, but it cannot tell what the complaints are about.","Domain 1: Natural language processing",[null,"Computer vision analyzes images; the emails are text.","Anomaly detection flags unusual values in data, not topics in written messages.","Image generation creates new pictures and does not analyze existing text."],1],["x3",0,1,"A payroll system must calculate tax using fixed formulas written in law, with exactly the same result every time. What is the best approach?",["A machine learning regression model trained on past payslips","Traditional rule-based code that applies the formulas","A generative AI model that writes each calculation","A clustering model that groups employees by salary"],1,"When the rules are known and must be applied exactly, ordinary code is simpler and more reliable than AI. A regression model is tempting because it predicts numbers, but it only estimates what the formulas give precisely.","Domain 1: When AI is not needed",["A learned model approximates results and can be slightly wrong, while the formulas are already known exactly.",null,"Generative output can vary between runs, which is unacceptable for exact tax results.","Grouping employees does not calculate tax."],2],["x4",0,1,"A résumé screening model rates women lower than men with the same experience. The training data came mostly from past hires who were men. What is the most likely cause?",["The model has too few layers to understand résumés","The screening app was deployed in the wrong Azure region","Women's résumés are longer, so they use more tokens","Biased, unrepresentative training data"],3,"Models reproduce patterns in their training data, so skewed historical data leads to unfair outcomes, a fairness issue. Model complexity is a tempting explanation, but a bigger model trained on the same data would learn the same bias.","Domain 1: Fairness and training data",["Model size does not explain a consistent gap between groups with equal experience.","Region affects where the app runs, not which candidates it favors.","Document length is not what produces this group-level bias.",null],2],["x5",0,1,"A fitness app lets users choose whether their workout data may be used to improve its AI features, and stores the data encrypted. Which responsible AI principle does this support most directly?",["Inclusiveness","Fairness","Privacy and security","Reliability and safety"],2,"Giving users control over their data and protecting it are core to privacy and security. Fairness is sometimes confused with it, but fairness concerns unequal outcomes for groups.","Domain 1: Privacy and security",["Inclusiveness is about designing for people of all abilities, not about consent and data protection.","Fairness is about treating groups of people equitably, not about how their data is protected.",null,"Reliability and safety is about the system behaving consistently and safely, not about data consent."],1],["x6",0,1,"A developer types 'write a function that checks whether a number is prime' and receives working code. Which AI workload is this?",["Generative AI","Knowledge mining","Anomaly detection","Optical character recognition"],0,"Creating new content such as code from a natural language prompt is generative AI. Knowledge mining is tempting because it also works with text, but it only finds and organizes content that already exists.","Domain 1: Generative AI workloads",[null,"Knowledge mining makes existing content searchable; it does not write new code.","Anomaly detection flags unusual data and does not produce code from a description.","OCR reads text from images, not writing code from a request."],1],["x7",0,1,"A library wants to turn photos of old printed pages into editable digital text. Which workload does this belong to?",["Conversational AI, using a chatbot","Computer vision, using optical character recognition","Machine learning regression","Speech recognition"],1,"Reading printed or handwritten text from images is OCR, part of computer vision. Speech recognition also produces text, but its input is audio rather than images.","Domain 1: Computer vision workloads",["A chatbot talks with users; it does not read text from images.",null,"Regression predicts numeric values and cannot read printed words.","Speech recognition converts spoken audio, not printed pages, into text."],1],["x8",0,1,"A medical chatbot sometimes gives confident but incorrect dosage advice. Which responsible AI principle is most directly at risk?",["Inclusiveness","Transparency","Privacy and security","Reliability and safety"],3,"A system that gives unsafe, wrong answers in a high-stakes setting fails reliability and safety. Transparency is tempting because users should know the bot can be wrong, but the core risk is the unsafe behavior.","Domain 1: Reliability and safety",["The problem is harmful wrong answers, not whether people of all abilities can use the bot.","Explaining how the bot works would not fix the incorrect dosage advice itself.","No personal data is being exposed; the issue is unsafe output.",null],2],["x9",0,1,"A hospital uses AI to flag X-rays that might show a fracture. What is the most responsible way to use the output?",["Treat every flag as a confirmed diagnosis and start treatment","A clinician reviews each flag before any decision","Hide the AI's involvement from the clinicians","Stop reviewing X-rays the model does not flag"],1,"Keeping a human expert in the loop for high-stakes decisions catches model errors. Treating flags as diagnoses is tempting for speed, but it removes that safeguard.","Domain 1: Human oversight",["The model can be wrong, and acting on every flag without review could harm patients.",null,"Hiding AI use removes the chance for experts to question its output.","Unflagged images could still contain fractures the model missed."],1],["x10",0,1,"A video platform adds automatic captions so that deaf and hard-of-hearing viewers can follow every video. Which responsible AI principle does this reflect?",["Inclusiveness","Accountability","Privacy and security","Transparency"],0,"Designing AI features so that people with disabilities can take part is inclusiveness. Transparency is a common mix-up, but it is about explaining the system, not widening access.","Domain 1: Inclusiveness",[null,"Accountability is about who answers for the system, not about serving people with different abilities.","Captions do not change how personal data is protected.","Transparency explains how a system works; captions make content accessible."],1],["x11",0,1,"A customer service chat begins with 'You are chatting with an AI assistant; it can make mistakes.' Which responsible AI principle does this support?",["Fairness","Inclusiveness","Accountability","Transparency"],3,"Telling users they are interacting with AI and that it has limits is transparency. Accountability is related but is about ownership and oversight behind the scenes.","Domain 1: Transparency",["The message does not change how different groups are treated.","The notice is about disclosure, not about access for people with different abilities.","Accountability concerns who is answerable for the system, not what users are told.",null],1],["x12",0,2,"A dataset for predicting apartment rent has columns for floor area, number of bedrooms, distance to the metro and monthly rent. Which columns are the features?",["Monthly rent only","All four columns, including monthly rent","Floor area, bedrooms and metro distance","Only the number of bedrooms"],2,"Features are the input values used to make a prediction, and the label is the value being predicted. Including monthly rent is the tempting mistake, but it is the label.","Domain 2: Features and labels",["Monthly rent is the value being predicted, so it is the label.","Features exclude the label; including rent would leak the answer into the inputs.",null,"Bedrooms is a feature, but floor area and distance are features too."],1],["x13",0,2,"A team trains a model on thousands of past loan applications, each marked as repaid or defaulted. What kind of machine learning is this?",["Unsupervised learning","Reinforcement learning","Clustering","Supervised learning"],3,"Training on examples whose outcome is known is supervised learning. Clustering is tempting because it also groups items, but it works without labels.","Domain 2: Supervised learning",["Unsupervised learning uses data without known outcomes, but these applications are labeled.","Reinforcement learning learns from rewards through trial and error, not from labeled examples.","Clustering is an unsupervised technique that groups unlabeled data.",null],1],["x14",0,2,"Two house-price models have the same mean absolute error, but model B has a much higher root mean squared error. What does this suggest?",["Model B is better because RMSE is higher","Model B makes a few large errors","Model B is overfitting its classification labels","Both models explain the same share of variance"],1,"RMSE squares errors before averaging, so a few large mistakes raise it far more than MAE. Reading a higher RMSE as better is the tempting error, since lower error is always better.","Domain 2: Regression metrics",["For error metrics, lower is better, so a higher RMSE is worse.",null,"These are regression metrics; the models do not predict classes.","MAE and RMSE do not show explained variance; that is R squared."],3],["x15",0,2,"Of all the emails a filter marked as spam, 95% really were spam. Which metric does this describe?",["Precision","Recall","Mean absolute error","Coefficient of determination"],0,"Precision is the proportion of positive predictions that were correct. Recall is the usual mix-up; it asks how many actual positives were found.","Domain 2: Precision",[null,"Recall is the share of all real spam that was caught, not the share of flagged emails that were spam.","MAE is a regression metric for numeric predictions.","R squared measures explained variance in regression, not classification quality."],2],["x16",0,2,"A model performs poorly on both its training data and its validation data. What is the most likely problem?",["Overfitting: the model memorized the training data","Data leakage from the validation set","Underfitting: the model is too simple","The validation set is too large"],2,"Poor results on both training and validation data point to a model that has not learned enough, called underfitting. Overfitting is tempting, but it shows strong training scores with weak validation scores.","Domain 2: Underfitting",["An overfit model does well on training data, which this model does not.","Leakage usually makes results look too good, not poor everywhere.",null,"A larger validation set gives a more reliable estimate; it does not lower training performance."],2],["x17",0,2,"A team has 20,000 unlabeled product photos and needs people to tag them before training a model in Azure Machine Learning. Which feature helps organize this work?",["Automated machine learning","A batch endpoint","A data labeling project","The Responsible AI dashboard"],2,"Data labeling projects let people tag images or text, track progress and export labeled data for training. Automated ML is tempting, but it starts only once labels exist.","Domain 2: Data labeling",["Automated ML needs labeled data already; it does not manage human tagging.","A batch endpoint scores data with a trained model and does not collect labels.",null,"The dashboard analyzes a trained model's behavior, not labeling work."],2],["x18",0,2,"A training job in Azure Machine Learning must scale out across several machines and scale back to zero when idle. What compute should it use?",["A compute instance","A real-time endpoint","A data labeling project","A compute cluster"],3,"Compute clusters are pools of VMs that scale out for jobs and can scale down to zero nodes. A compute instance is tempting because it is also compute, but it is one machine for development.","Domain 2: Compute clusters",["A compute instance is a single development machine, not an autoscaling pool for jobs.","Real-time endpoints serve predictions from a trained model; they do not run training jobs.","Labeling projects organize tagging work and provide no compute for training.",null],2],["x19",0,2,"Every night, a retailer must score two million customer records with a trained churn model. Immediate responses are not needed. How should the model be deployed?",["As a batch endpoint","As a real-time endpoint called once per record","As a compute instance running a notebook by hand","As a data labeling project"],0,"Batch endpoints process large volumes of data asynchronously, which suits scheduled scoring. A real-time endpoint also works but is designed for low-latency single requests.","Domain 2: Batch endpoints",[null,"Two million separate real-time calls is inefficient when no instant answer is needed.","Running a notebook manually is not a reliable deployment for nightly scoring.","Labeling collects human tags and does not score records."],2],["x20",0,2,"A data scientist runs k-means clustering on customer data with k set to 4. What does the value 4 mean?",["The model will use four features only","The algorithm will form four clusters","Training will run for four epochs","Four labels must be supplied for each customer"],1,"In k-means, k is the number of clusters the algorithm groups the data into. Thinking k means labels is tempting, but clustering works without labels.","Domain 2: K-means clustering",["k sets the number of groups, not how many input columns are used.",null,"k-means has no epoch setting called k; it iterates until clusters stabilize.","Clustering is unsupervised, so no labels are supplied."],2],["x21",0,2,"Only 1% of transactions are fraud. A model predicts 'not fraud' for every transaction and reaches 99% accuracy. Why is accuracy misleading here?",["Accuracy above 95% always means the model is overfitting","Accuracy can only be used for regression models","The validation set must have been larger than the training set","The model never catches fraud, so its recall for fraud is zero"],3,"With imbalanced classes, always predicting the majority class gives high accuracy while missing every positive, so recall or precision are more useful. Overfitting is a tempting guess, but this model has learned nothing at all.","Domain 2: Imbalanced data and accuracy",["High accuracy alone does not indicate overfitting; the problem is the class imbalance.","Accuracy is a classification metric; it just hides failure on rare classes.","Set sizes do not explain why predicting one class yields high accuracy.",null],3],["x22",0,2,"A team retrains a model every month and must keep track of each version and which one is in production. What should they use in Azure Machine Learning?",["Save each model file on a developer's laptop","Create a new workspace every month","Register each model version in the workspace","Store the models in a data labeling project"],2,"Registering models in the workspace gives each one a name and version with its metadata, so the team can see what is deployed. A new workspace per month is tempting for separation, but it defeats central tracking.","Domain 2: Model registry",["Local copies are hard to track, share and audit across a team.","Separate workspaces scatter versions instead of tracking them in one place.",null,"Labeling projects hold tagged data, not trained model versions."],2],["x23",0,3,"A stock photo site wants each image labeled with keywords such as 'beach', 'sunset' and 'people' so users can search for them. Which Azure AI Vision capability fits?",["Optical character recognition","Image tagging","Face identification","Smart cropping"],1,"Tagging returns a set of content keywords for an image, ideal for search. OCR is tempting because it also returns words, but only words that are written in the picture.","Domain 3: Image tagging",["OCR reads text in the image, not keywords describing its content.",null,"Face identification recognizes specific people, which is not what the site needs.","Smart cropping picks a thumbnail region and does not produce keywords."],1],["x24",0,3,"An expense app must read the merchant name, date and total from photos of shop receipts and return them as named fields. Which service fits best?",["Document Intelligence prebuilt receipt model","Azure AI Vision image tagging","Azure AI Language key phrase extraction on the photo","Azure AI Face detection"],0,"The prebuilt receipt model returns structured fields such as merchant, date and total. Plain OCR would give the text, but the app would still have to work out which value is which.","Domain 3: Receipts",[null,"Tags describe what is in the photo, not the merchant or total as fields.","Key phrases work on text you already have and do not return receipt fields.","Face detection finds faces; receipts contain none of the needed data."],2],["x25",0,3,"A street-view service must find every face in its photos so it can blur them. It does not need to know who anyone is. Which capability is needed?",["Face identification","Face detection","Image classification","Optical character recognition"],1,"Face detection returns the location of each face without identifying the person, which is all blurring needs. Face identification is tempting, but it adds recognition that the service must not do.","Domain 3: Face detection",["Identification matches faces to known people, which is not needed and requires approval.",null,"Classification labels the whole image and does not locate each face.","OCR finds text such as signs, not faces."],2],["x26",0,3,"To a computer vision model, what is a digital color photo?",["A list of the objects that appear in the scene","A sentence that describes what the photo shows","A grid of pixels, each stored as numeric color values","A set of keywords added by the camera"],2,"An image is an array of pixels, and each pixel holds numbers for its color channels, which models process. A list of objects is tempting, but that is the output of analysis, not the input.","Domain 3: How computers see images",["Object lists are something a model produces, not how the image is stored.","Descriptions are generated from images; the image itself is numbers.",null,"Keywords are metadata at most; the model reads pixel values."],1],["x27",0,3,"A manufacturer needs to recognize its own specialized machine parts, which prebuilt image analysis does not identify. What should it do?",["Call the prebuilt image tagging feature more often","Use Azure AI Translator on the photos","Use Azure AI Face with each part's photo","Train a custom model with labeled photos"],3,"Custom vision models learn new classes from your own labeled images. Repeating prebuilt tagging is tempting, but a prebuilt model only knows the general objects it was trained on.","Domain 3: Custom vision models",["Calling the same prebuilt model again will not teach it new part types.","Translator works with text and cannot recognize objects in images.","Face analyzes human faces only.",null],2],["x28",0,3,"A training team has hundreds of recorded videos and wants to search them for spoken words, on-screen text and the topics covered. Which service fits best?",["Azure AI Video Indexer","Azure AI Translator","Azure AI Document Intelligence","Azure AI Face identification"],0,"Video Indexer extracts transcripts, on-screen text, topics and more from video so it can be searched. Document Intelligence is tempting because it extracts text, but it works on documents and forms.","Domain 3: Video analysis",[null,"Translator translates text but does not index video content.","Document Intelligence extracts fields from documents, not from videos.","Face identification recognizes people and does not transcribe speech or extract topics."],2],["x29",0,3,"Document Intelligence returns an invoice total with a confidence score of 0.42. What is the most sensible next step?",["Send the invoice to a person to check that field","Accept the value because any score above 0 is valid","Delete the invoice from the system","Retrain the prebuilt invoice model yourself"],0,"Confidence scores show how sure the model is, and low-confidence fields are a good trigger for human review. Accepting any positive score is tempting for automation, but it lets errors through.","Domain 3: Confidence scores",[null,"A low score means the model is unsure, so accepting it risks wrong data.","Discarding the document loses real data that a quick check could confirm.","Prebuilt models are not retrained by customers."],2],["x30",0,3,"A vision model returns, for each item it finds, a label such as 'bicycle' and the coordinates of a box around it. Which technique produces this output?",["Image classification","Object detection","Optical character recognition","Image captioning"],1,"Object detection outputs a class label plus a bounding box for each detected object. Image classification is the common mix-up, but it describes the image as a whole.","Domain 3: Object detection output",["Classification gives labels for the whole image, without boxes around items.",null,"OCR returns text and its location, not labeled objects.","Captioning returns a sentence about the image, not a box per item."],1],["x31",0,3,"An accessibility tool must describe several different parts of a busy street photo, each with its own short description and location. Which capability fits?",["A single image caption","Image tagging","Dense captions","Face detection"],2,"Dense captions generate descriptions for multiple regions of an image, each with a bounding box. A single caption is tempting, but it summarizes the whole picture in one sentence.","Domain 3: Dense captions",["A caption gives one sentence for the whole image, not one per region.","Tags are single keywords without region descriptions.",null,"Face detection only locates faces, not the other parts of the scene."],2],["x32",0,3,"What does a team need to train an image classification model that tells cats from dogs?",["A single photo of each animal","Text descriptions of cats and dogs with no images","Unlabeled images only","Many images, each labeled as cat or dog"],3,"Image classification is supervised learning, so it needs many examples labeled with the correct class. Unlabeled images are tempting because they are easy to collect, but the model would have nothing to learn the classes from.","Domain 3: Training data for classification",["One example per class is far too little for a model to learn reliable patterns.","An image classifier learns from images, not from text alone.","Classification is supervised, so the images need labels.",null],1],["x33",0,3,"A car rental desk wants to read the name, date of birth and document number from customers' passports automatically, without training. Which option fits?",["The Azure AI Face identification feature","A custom text classification model","The Document Intelligence prebuilt ID document model","The Azure AI Vision tagging feature"],2,"The prebuilt ID document model extracts standard fields from passports and many driver's licenses. Face identification is tempting because passports have photos, but it does not read the text fields.","Domain 3: Identity documents",["Face identification compares faces; it does not read the printed passport fields.","Text classification labels documents by category and does not extract fields.",null,"Tags describe image content and do not return identity fields."],1],["x34",0,4,"Before analyzing reviews, a text pipeline reduces 'running', 'ran' and 'runs' to the common form 'run'. What is this step called?",["Stemming or lemmatization","Tokenization","Named entity recognition","Sentiment analysis"],0,"Stemming and lemmatization reduce different forms of a word to a common base so they are counted together. Tokenization is the tempting choice because it is also a preprocessing step, but it only splits text.","Domain 4: Text normalization",[null,"Tokenization splits text into units such as words; it does not merge word forms.","NER finds names of people, places and organizations, not word roots.","Sentiment analysis rates opinion, not word forms."],3],["x35",0,4,"In a set of news articles, the word 'election' appears many times in one article but rarely in the others. How does TF-IDF treat this word for that article?",["It scores it low because it is rare across the collection","It removes it as a stop word","It gives it the same score in every article","It scores it high for that article"],3,"TF-IDF rewards words that are frequent in one document but uncommon across the collection. Thinking rarity lowers the score is the tempting mistake; inverse document frequency does the opposite.","Domain 4: TF-IDF",["Rarity across documents raises the IDF part of the score, not lowers it.","Stop words are very common words such as 'the', not distinctive ones.","TF-IDF depends on how often a word appears in each document, so scores differ.",null],3],["x36",0,4,"A brand wants its app to speak in a unique synthetic voice based on recordings of its hired voice actor. Which Azure AI Speech capability supports this?",["Speech to text with a custom model","Custom neural voice","Speech translation","Keyword recognition"],1,"Custom neural voice builds a synthetic voice from recordings of a consenting voice talent, and access is limited to approved uses. A custom speech model is the tempting mix-up, but it is for recognizing speech.","Domain 4: Custom neural voice",["A custom speech model improves recognition of audio; it does not create a new voice.",null,"Speech translation converts speech between languages, not into a new branded voice.","Keyword recognition listens for a wake word and does not synthesize speech."],2],["x37",0,4,"An engineering firm finds that Translator keeps using general words instead of its approved technical terms. It has many previously translated documents. What should it use?",["Language detection before each request","Key phrase extraction on the source text","Custom Translator trained on its translated documents","Speech translation instead of text translation"],2,"Custom Translator builds a translation system from your own parallel documents so it uses your terminology. Language detection is tempting as a first step, but it has no effect on word choice.","Domain 4: Custom Translator",["Detecting the language does not change which terms are chosen.","Key phrases identify topics but do not affect translation choices.",null,"Changing to speech input does not teach the service the firm's terminology."],2],["x38",0,4,"A company must translate hundreds of Word and PDF files and keep their original layout and formatting. Which Azure AI Translator feature fits?",["Text translation, pasting each file's text into the request","Document translation","Speech translation","Language detection"],1,"Document translation processes whole files, such as Word and PDF, while preserving their structure and formatting. Text translation is tempting because it translates the same words, but it only handles plain text.","Domain 4: Document translation",["Pasting plain text loses the documents' formatting and does not scale to hundreds of files.",null,"Speech translation works with spoken audio, not document files.","Language detection identifies the language but does not translate anything."],2],["x39",0,4,"In the utterance 'Book a table for four in Lisbon tonight', what are 'four', 'Lisbon' and 'tonight' in a conversational language understanding model?",["Entities","Intents","Utterances","Key phrases"],0,"Entities are the specific pieces of information in an utterance that the app needs to act on. Intents are the tempting choice, but the intent here is the booking goal as a whole.","Domain 4: Entities in CLU",[null,"The intent is the overall goal, such as booking a table, not the details inside the request.","The whole sentence is the utterance; these words are parts of it.","Key phrases come from a different text analysis feature, not from a CLU model."],1],["x40",0,4,"A smart home app must understand commands such as 'turn off the kitchen lights' and trigger the right action. Which Azure AI Language feature fits best?",["Custom question answering","Summarization","Language detection","Conversational language understanding"],3,"CLU predicts the intent, such as TurnOff, and entities, such as the room, so the app can act. Question answering is tempting because it also handles user input, but it matches questions to fixed answers.","Domain 4: Question answering vs CLU",["Question answering returns stored answers to questions; it does not extract commands and their details.","Summarization shortens long text and does not trigger actions.","Language detection only identifies which language the command is in.",null],2],["x41",0,4,"A call center records thousands of calls each day and needs written transcripts by the next morning. Which Azure AI Speech approach fits best?",["Real-time speech to text on a live microphone","Text to speech with a neural voice","Batch transcription of the stored recordings","Speaker-independent keyword recognition"],2,"Batch transcription processes large sets of recorded audio asynchronously, which suits overnight work. Real-time speech to text is tempting because it also transcribes, but it is designed for live streams.","Domain 4: Batch transcription",["Real-time recognition is for live audio, not thousands of stored files processed overnight.","Text to speech creates audio from text, the opposite of what is needed.",null,"Keyword recognition only listens for a wake word; it does not transcribe calls."],2],["x42",0,4,"Sentiment analysis labels a review as 'mixed'. What does this mean?",["The service could not detect the review's language","It has both positive and negative sentences","The review is completely neutral","The review was written in two languages"],1,"A mixed label means the document has sentences with positive and with negative sentiment. Neutral is the tempting confusion, but neutral means no strong opinion either way.","Domain 4: Sentiment results",["Language problems produce errors or unknown results, not a mixed label.",null,"Neutral means no clear opinion; mixed means both positive and negative opinions are present.","Mixed describes sentiment, not the number of languages."],2],["x43",0,4,"A business analyst wants to try sentiment analysis and entity recognition on sample text in a browser, without writing code. What can they use?",["The Azure Machine Learning designer","Azure Storage Explorer","The Azure activity log","The Azure AI Foundry portal or Language Studio"],3,"The Foundry portal and Language Studio provide no-code pages to try Language features on your own text. The Machine Learning designer is tempting because it is also no-code, but it is for building custom models.","Domain 4: Trying language features",["The designer builds ML pipelines and is not the place to try prebuilt Language features.","Storage Explorer manages files and blobs; it does not run language analysis.","The activity log records management actions and cannot analyze text.",null],1],["x44",0,4,"A smart speaker hears 'Set an alarm for 6 a.m.' In conversational language understanding, what is 'SetAlarm'?",["The intent","An entity","A key phrase","A language code"],0,"An intent represents what the user wants to do, and CLU predicts it for each utterance. Entity is the tempting answer, but entities are the details, such as the time.","Domain 4: Intents",[null,"The time 6 a.m. would be an entity; SetAlarm is the goal of the request.","Key phrases are topics extracted by a separate feature, not the predicted goal.","Language codes identify languages such as en-US, not user goals."],1],["x45",0,5,"During a very long chat, a model starts ignoring details the user gave at the very beginning. What is the most likely reason?",["The temperature was set too low","The content filter removed the early messages","The model was retrained during the chat","It exceeded the model's context window"],3,"A model can only consider a limited number of tokens at once, so older parts of a long conversation may be dropped. Blaming temperature is tempting, but temperature affects randomness, not memory.","Domain 5: Context window",["Low temperature makes answers more focused; it does not make the model forget earlier messages.","Content filters block harmful content, not ordinary earlier details.","Models are not retrained in the middle of a conversation.",null],2],["x46",0,5,"A marketing team wants a model to brainstorm many varied and surprising slogan ideas. How should temperature be set?",["At 0, for the most predictable output","It does not matter; temperature only affects speed","Higher, to allow more varied output","Negative, to avoid repeated slogans"],2,"Higher temperature makes the model choose less likely tokens more often, producing more varied ideas. Setting it to 0 is tempting for quality, but it gives the most predictable, least varied output.","Domain 5: Temperature for creativity",["A temperature of 0 makes output focused and repetitive, the opposite of brainstorming.","Temperature controls randomness, not response speed.",null,"Temperature is not set to negative values, and lowering it reduces variety."],1],["x47",0,5,"A user asks a model 'Classify this review as positive or negative' and includes no examples. What kind of prompt is this?",["Few-shot prompting","Zero-shot prompting","Fine-tuning","Retrieval augmented generation"],1,"A zero-shot prompt asks the model to perform a task with instructions only and no examples. Few-shot is the tempting mix-up, but it requires examples in the prompt.","Domain 5: Zero-shot prompting",["Few-shot prompts include example inputs and outputs, which this prompt does not.",null,"Fine-tuning retrains a model on data; it is not a single prompt.","RAG adds retrieved documents to the prompt, which is not happening here."],2],["x48",0,5,"A model often gets multi-step word problems wrong. Which prompt change is most likely to help?",["Ask for step-by-step reasoning before the answer","Ask it to answer with a single word and no explanation","Raise the temperature to the maximum","Remove the system message entirely"],0,"Asking for step-by-step reasoning (chain-of-thought) often improves results on multi-step problems. A one-word answer is tempting for brevity but gives the model no space to reason.","Domain 5: Step-by-step reasoning",[null,"Forcing a one-word answer removes room for reasoning and usually hurts accuracy.","More randomness tends to make reasoning less reliable.","Removing guidance does not improve multi-step reasoning."],2],["x49",0,5,"A company wants a model to always reply in its distinctive brand tone and format. The facts it uses rarely change, and it has thousands of example replies. Which approach fits best?",["Build a RAG index over the example replies","Fine-tune a model on the example replies","Set the temperature to 2","Use a larger context window with no examples"],1,"Fine-tuning is well suited to teaching a consistent style or format from many examples. RAG is tempting because it uses your data, but it is best for grounding answers in changing facts.","Domain 5: Fine-tuning vs RAG",["RAG supplies facts at query time; it is less effective at teaching a consistent style.",null,"A high temperature adds randomness and makes tone less consistent.","More context space does nothing unless you fill it with guidance or examples."],3],["x50",0,5,"A semantic search app compares a query embedding with document embeddings. Which measure is commonly used to decide how similar two embeddings are?",["Cosine similarity","The number of characters in each document","Mean absolute error","The model's temperature setting"],0,"Cosine similarity measures the angle between vectors, so embeddings with similar meaning score close to 1. Mean absolute error is tempting because it also compares numbers, but it is a regression evaluation metric.","Domain 5: Comparing embeddings",[null,"Length says nothing about whether two texts share meaning.","MAE evaluates numeric predictions against true values, not similarity between vectors.","Temperature affects text generation, not how vectors are compared."],3],["x51",0,5,"GPT-style language models generate text one token at a time. Which part of the transformer architecture are they mainly built from?",["Encoder blocks only, as in BERT-style models","Convolutional layers designed for images","Decision trees combined in a forest","Decoder blocks"],3,"GPT models are decoder-only transformers that predict the next token from the previous ones. Encoder-only models such as BERT are the tempting choice, but they produce representations for understanding tasks rather than generating text.","Domain 5: Transformer architecture",["Encoder-only models are designed to understand text, not to generate it token by token.","Convolutional layers are mainly used for image models, not GPT text generation.","Decision forests are classic ML models, not transformer components.",null],3],["x52",0,5,"A developer picked a chat model in the Microsoft Foundry model catalog. What must they do before an app can send it prompts?",["Fine-tune the model on company data","Train the model from scratch","Create a deployment of the model","Create an Azure AI Search index"],2,"Apps call a model through a deployment, which gives it an endpoint and a name to use in requests. Fine-tuning is tempting as a first step, but it is optional.","Domain 5: Deploying a model",["Fine-tuning is optional; a base model can be used once deployed.","Catalog models are already trained, so training from scratch is not needed.",null,"A search index is only needed for RAG, not to call a model."],1],["x53",0,5,"A RAG app retrieves web pages that might contain hidden text telling the model to ignore its rules. Which safety feature is designed to detect this?",["Prompt shields","Groundedness detection","Custom text classification","Speech translation"],0,"Prompt shields detect attempts to override a model's instructions, including attacks hidden in documents. Groundedness detection is tempting because it also inspects RAG content, but it checks answers against sources.","Domain 5: Prompt injection",[null,"Groundedness checks whether answers are supported by sources, not whether sources contain hidden instructions.","A classifier you train sorts text into categories and is not a built-in defense against injected instructions.","Speech translation converts spoken language and has no role in this problem."],2],["x54",0,5,"The content filters used with Azure OpenAI models rate harmful content in categories such as hate and violence. How is each category rated?",["By a yes-or-no flag with no levels","By a star rating from 1 to 5","By the number of tokens in the text","By severity level: safe, low, medium or high"],3,"Each harm category is rated by severity, and filters block content at or above a chosen threshold. A simple yes-or-no flag is tempting, but it would not allow adjustable thresholds.","Domain 5: Content filter severity",["The filters use graded severity levels, which is what lets you set thresholds.","Star ratings are not used; severity levels are.","Token count measures length, not how harmful content is.",null],2],["x55",0,5,"An evaluation checks whether each chatbot answer actually addresses the question the user asked, regardless of the source documents. Which metric is this?",["Groundedness","Fluency","Relevance","Latency"],2,"Relevance measures how well a response addresses the user's question. Groundedness is the tempting choice because it is common in RAG evaluation, but it compares answers with the sources.","Domain 5: Relevance metric",["Groundedness checks support from source documents, which this evaluation ignores.","Fluency rates grammar and readability, not whether the question was answered.",null,"Latency measures response time, not answer quality."],3],["x56",0,5,"A user uploads a photo of a broken appliance and asks a chat app 'What part is damaged?' What kind of model can handle both the image and the question?",["A text-only language model","A multimodal model","An embeddings model","A speech to text model"],1,"Multimodal models accept more than one type of input, such as images and text, in the same request. A text-only model is tempting because the question is text, but it cannot see the photo.","Domain 5: Multimodal models",["A text-only model cannot take the photo as input.",null,"Embeddings models turn input into vectors; they do not answer questions.","Speech to text transcribes audio and cannot look at images."],1],["x57",0,5,"A team is estimating the cost of a chat app that uses a pay-as-you-go Azure OpenAI deployment. What mainly drives the cost?",["The number of users registered in the app","Input and output tokens processed","The number of days the deployment exists","The size of the app's source code"],1,"Pay-as-you-go model usage is billed per token for both the prompt and the response. Counting days is tempting from other Azure services, but that applies to reserved capacity, not standard deployments.","Domain 5: Token-based pricing",["Registered users cost nothing by themselves; usage is billed by tokens.",null,"Pay-as-you-go deployments are billed on usage rather than on how long they exist.","Code size has no effect on model charges."],1],["x58",0,5,"An HR agent built in Foundry Agent Service must answer questions from a set of uploaded policy PDFs. Which kind of tool gives it this knowledge?",["Code interpreter","A text to speech tool","File search over the uploaded documents","An image generation tool"],2,"File search indexes uploaded files so the agent can retrieve relevant passages and cite them. Code interpreter is tempting because it can open files, but it is meant for running code and analyzing data.","Domain 5: Agent knowledge tools",["Code interpreter runs code for calculations and data analysis, not document question answering.","Text to speech produces audio and does not help the agent find answers.",null,"Image generation creates pictures and does not read documents."],2],["x59",0,5,"A team runs automated evaluations to find out how often its chatbot produces harmful or ungrounded answers. Which stage of Microsoft's responsible generative AI process is this?",["Identify","Mitigate","Operate","Measure"],3,"The measure stage quantifies how often identified harms occur, often with evaluations and testing. Identify is the tempting choice, but it only names the possible harms.","Domain 5: Measure stage",["Identify is about listing potential harms, not quantifying how often they occur.","Mitigate applies fixes such as filters and prompts after the harms are measured.","Operate covers deployment and ongoing monitoring after release.",null],2],["x60",0,5,"A news site publishes illustrations created with an image generation model. Which practice best supports responsible use?",["Clearly disclose that the images were generated by AI","Present the images as real photographs","Remove all metadata that shows how the images were made","Use the highest temperature so images look unique"],0,"Labeling AI-generated content, sometimes with content credentials, supports transparency and helps readers judge what they see. Removing metadata is tempting for tidiness, but it hides the images' origin.","Domain 5: Disclosing AI-generated content",[null,"Passing off generated images as real photos misleads readers.","Stripping provenance information hides the AI origin instead of disclosing it.","Temperature settings have nothing to do with honest disclosure."],2]]);
